[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known: "the parser reads dates month
first, and our tenants write the day first." That is true for most of its reports, not all. Read by
tenant and by what each report says, #4471 holds three problems. Each now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The fix queued for #4471 fixes the parser only. The other two problems stay after it ships.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  issues={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/CasterShell/support-kb-4471/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/CasterShell/support-kb-4471/issues/2',
    oneDay: 'https://github.com/CasterShell/support-kb-4471/issues/3',
  }}
/>

- **parser.** The shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file writes the
  day first. 7 April becomes 4 July.
- **hint.** The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Clients type what it
  says, and the form reads the date day first. The parser never sees the flip, so its fix cannot
  reach these.
- **oneDay.** The date moves by one day: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got
  Monday." No day/month flip does that. The desk noted these reports in `desk-notes.json` and
  filed them under the parser anyway.

## Why one ticket hid them

- **Intake tags guess.** `intakeTag` comes from the reporter's browser. The cause comes from the
  tenant: where they are and which booking form they run (`causes.ts`). Counting by tag gives the
  wrong size for the hint problem.
- **The cause table counts the one-day moves as parser.** It footnotes them and adds them to the
  parser row, so the table showed two causes where there are three.
- **Merging hides the rest.** One ticket with one count reads as one bug. The table the desk
  already had, for the same ticket:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## For the desk

- Close #4471 against the parser fix only. Track the hint and one-day problems on their own
  tickets.
- Before merging a report into a big ticket, check its tenant and channel, not its tag.
- A report that does not read as a flip is not a duplicate, even with the same subject.

